<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altamaha River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Altamaha River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, is formed by the meeting of the Oconee and Ocmulgee rivers and flows into the Atlantic near Darien. The charter of 1732 set it as the southern limit of the Province of Georgia, facing Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, and Scottish Highlanders settled at its mouth in 1736 to guard the frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altamaha-river.jpg" alt="Altamaha River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bubba73 (Jud McCranie) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altamaha_River_from_bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Altamaha begins near Lumber City and flows generally eastward for 137 miles (220 km); counted with its longest tributary, the Ocmulgee, it is 470 miles (760 km) long. Its basin covers about 14,000 square miles (36,000 km2), among the larger basins of the Atlantic coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. No dams stand on the river itself, and it runs through thinly peopled country, joined by the Ohoopee River in Tattnall County. The town of Darien lies just north of its mouth at Altamaha Sound, and St. Simons Island lies on the south side of its estuary, which covers about 26 square miles (67 km2).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timucua">Timucua</a> people lived as far north as the Altamaha, and the Utinahica lived along the river, where the Spanish mission of Santa Isabel de Utinahica was founded around 1610 near its source. Along the coast of Spanish Florida the river divided the missionary provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guale">Guale</a> and Mocama. Fort Caroline, built by the French in 1564, was probably near its mouth, though the site has not been excavated; in 1565 Spanish soldiers under Pedro Menéndez killed some 143 French Huguenot Protestants there.</p><p>Late in the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamasee">Yamasee</a> under Chief Altamaha, from whom the river takes its name, settled near the mouth. In 1738 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a> wrote to the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> that he had taken down Fort King George on the river, which had offended the Spanish.</p><p>Riverboats long used the Altamaha to reach the towns and plantations along it, and in the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> rafts up to about 250 feet (76 m) long carried logs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and Darien for shipment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. John Bartram found the Franklin tree, now extinct in the wild, beside the river in 1765, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-goldsmith">Oliver Goldsmith</a> named the river in his poem The Deserted Village of 1770.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river remains almost entirely in its natural state, crossed by roads only five times, with old-growth cypress swamps and at least 120 rare or endangered species in its watershed. It was designated a bioreserve in 1991. The Plant Hatch nuclear power plant stands on its southern bank in Appling County.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Altamaha River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1732 George II granted a charter to a board of Trustees led by James Oglethorpe for the land between the Savannah and <b>Altamaha</b> rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-georgia">Province of Georgia</a></li></ul></section>
